<TopicPage slug="subjects/music/expert/music-cognition">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Music cognition asks <strong>why sound organised this way moves human brains that way</strong>
    — the junction of music theory, psychology and neuroscience. Its findings explain the theory
    lessons above from the listener's side: why consonance pleases, why expectation creates
    emotion, why some four-chord loops are irresistible, and why every culture dances.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four findings that connect theory to feeling" widget="equationStep" config={cognCfg} />

  <h2>Open questions with real depth</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Why does music exist?</strong> No obvious survival function. Candidates: sexual display (Darwin's own theory), social bonding (singing synchronises groups — evolutionary advantage), or a pleasurable by-product of auditory pattern-seeking. Unresolved — and fascinating at every branch.</li>
    <li><strong>Universal or cultural?</strong> Some features appear everywhere (dance, lullabies, tonal centres — a 2019 Harvard study of 315 societies confirmed), while emotional readings of specific intervals vary. Both nature and nurture, in proportions still being measured.</li>
    <li><strong>Music and language share machinery</strong> (pitch patterns, syntax-like expectation) but use different circuits — the overlap and divergence is an active research frontier with clinical uses (melodic therapy for stroke speech recovery).</li>
    <li><strong>Therapy is real:</strong> music therapy measurably helps Parkinson's gait, dementia recall and anxiety. The mechanisms are partly understood — entrainment, memory, reward — and the field is one of the best bridges between the music ladder and the neuroscience one.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Huron, Sweet Anticipation (2006); Salimpoor et al., Nature Neuroscience (2011); Mehr et al., Behavioral and Brain Sciences (2019); Savage et al., "Universal features of music".</p>
</TopicPage>
